      "dom_id": "question-84978",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Diana is choosing a $4$-digit passcode for her cellphone from the digits $\\{1,2,3,4,5,6\\},$ where no digit is used more than once. How many different passcodes are possible?",
      "worked_solution": "The number of permutations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{P}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $6$ digits, so $n=6.$ We want to select $4$ of those digits for the passcode, so we have $r=4.$ \n\nTherefore, the number of different passcodes is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{6}{P}_{4} & =\\frac{6!}{(6-4)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{6!}{2!} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\cdot 5\\cdot 4\\cdot 3\\cdot \\cancel{2!}}{\\cancel{2!}} \\\\  & =6\\cdot 5\\cdot 4\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =360.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
